# Scoped line-ending pins only — no repo-wide normalization.

# The fake-claude POSIX shim is exec'd by the kernel on Linux CI; a CRLF
# checkout would corrupt the `#!/bin/sh` shebang (exec format error), making
# Commander's `which claude` lookup fail silently. Pin LF regardless of the
# checkout machine's core.autocrlf.
tests/e2e/fixtures/fake-claude/claude text eol=lf
tests/e2e/fixtures/fake-claude/fake-claude.mjs text eol=lf

# Same protection for the fake-codex POSIX shim (kernel-exec'd on Linux CI).
tests/e2e/fixtures/fake-codex/codex text eol=lf
tests/e2e/fixtures/fake-codex/fake-codex.mjs text eol=lf

# Generated Commander tool-contract artifacts (tools.json / TOOLS.md) are
# regenerated by pnpm gen:tools / gen:tools:md and drift-checked in CI
# (pr.yml verify job). Pin LF so a Windows checkout with autocrlf=true can't
# rewrite them to CRLF and make the drift check falsely fail.
packages/shared/src/generated/tools.json text eol=lf
server/src/onboarding-assets/commander/TOOLS.md text eol=lf

# The agent instruction bundles are DELIVERED INTO A LINUX SANDBOX as an agent's
# system prompt (CLI-008 Unit B), so CRLF in them is wrong on its own terms and not
# merely a measurement nuisance. Same reasoning as the TOOLS.md pin above, which is
# why TOOLS.md was the only bundle file unaffected when a Windows checkout made
# `cli-008-unit-b-byte-source` measure 26,568 bytes where Linux CI measured 26,351
# (110 + 65 + 42 = 217 CRLF pairs across AGENTS/HEARTBEAT/SOUL). The blobs are
# already LF, so this changes no committed bytes -- it stops the working tree from
# diverging from what CI and the sandbox see.
server/src/onboarding-assets/**/AGENTS.md text eol=lf
server/src/onboarding-assets/**/HEARTBEAT.md text eol=lf
server/src/onboarding-assets/**/SOUL.md text eol=lf
server/src/onboarding-assets/**/TOOLS.md text eol=lf

# Generated AoA-native-skills catalog (pnpm gen:skills / gen:skills:check)
# and the eval snapshot committed alongside the commander-skill-triggering
# suite (Plan 2, Task 6) — same CRLF-drift risk as the tools.json artifacts
# above: a Windows checkout with autocrlf=true must not rewrite these to
# CRLF and desync from what the generator/suite committed.
server/src/services/internal-agent/generated/aoa-native-skills.json text eol=lf
server/src/eval/commander-skill-triggering/skills-snapshot.json text eol=lf

# Versioned connector-contract bytes are covered by manifest.sha256. Pin the
# inputs and manifest to LF so Windows core.autocrlf cannot invalidate hashes.
docs/contracts/mcp-connectors/v1/*.json text eol=lf
docs/contracts/mcp-connectors/v1/manifest.sha256 text eol=lf

# Versioned worker-protocol contract bytes (PRT-006) are covered by
# manifest.sha256 and byte-checked cross-platform in CI. Pin every file in the
# directory (conformance.json + operations.md + README.md + the manifest) to LF
# so a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true cannot invalidate the
# deterministic hashes.
docs/contracts/worker-protocol/v1/** text eol=lf

# The frozen independent worker-protocol v1 consumer baseline (PRT-007) is a
# hash-pinned fixture: its bundled dist, declaration tree, package metadata,
# dependency lock, and manifest are all covered by manifest.sha256 and recomputed
# by cross-version.test.ts + the frozen-consumer checker. Pin every file to LF so
# a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true cannot invalidate the deterministic
# hashes.
tests/fixtures/worker-protocol-consumers/v1/** text eol=lf

# This file's historical base blob contains NUL bytes. Treat the repaired
# source as LF text so PR review and future diffs remain inspectable.
server/src/realtime/live-events-ws.ts text eol=lf diff

# A SHEBANG FOLLOWED BY CRLF BREAKS AN IMPORT, though not an execution. vite/esbuild
# strips `#!...` up to the LF and leaves a stray CR behind, which V8 then rejects with
# "Invalid or unexpected token". Node's own loader handles it, so `node scripts/x.mjs`
# works fine and ONLY an import from a vitest suite fails — which is exactly why this was
# invisible on Linux CI (checked out LF) and red on every Windows checkout (autocrlf).
#
# It is not hypothetical: packages/worker-protocol/src/cross-version.test.ts imports
# scripts/check-frozen-worker-protocol-consumer.mjs, and that one CR made the frozen
# worker-protocol suite fail locally for anyone on Windows. Isolated by bisection —
# shebang+LF imports fine, shebang+CRLF does not, and the same file with its shebang
# removed imports fine.
#
# Pinned for the whole scripts/ directory rather than the one file, because every
# shebang-bearing executable has the same property and the next one to be imported by a
# test would fail the same way.
# `scripts/*.mjs` would match ONLY the top level — `*` does not cross a slash — while
# the reason above says "the whole scripts/ directory". `**` between slashes matches
# zero or more directories, so these cover both scripts/x.mjs and scripts/lib/x.mjs.
scripts/**/*.mjs text eol=lf
scripts/**/*.sh text eol=lf

# The distributed-execution foundation fixtures and architecture docs are copied
# verbatim into the mutation harness's temp --root and mutated with LF-based
# find-strings (check-distributed-execution-foundation.test.mjs). A CRLF Windows
# checkout makes those finds no-ops, so three mutation self-checks fail locally
# while Linux CI (LF) passes. Pin LF so the corpus mutates identically on every
# platform, and so the census `runs` flip is honest cross-platform. The index is
# already LF for every file here, so this changes only Windows working-tree bytes
# (Linux CI is unaffected). (foundation-suite-unrun)
tests/fixtures/distributed-execution/** text eol=lf
docs/architecture/distributed-execution-* text eol=lf

# WRK-017 — the D1 worker enrolment ticket is read by a Linux container and split on `.`
# by two independent decoders. The daemon's reader trims surrounding whitespace, so CRLF is
# survivable today; pin LF anyway so what a Windows checkout mounts is byte-identical to what
# CI mounts, and so the one-line-file assertion in docker/d1/__tests__ measures the same bytes
# everywhere. The file is already LF, so this changes no committed bytes.
docker/d1/worker-b.enrollment-ticket text eol=lf

# Drizzle migration files + their journal metadata are HASHED (sha256 of file bytes) to form
# the migration identity: loadRequiredMigrationIdentity (packages/db/src/client.ts) hashes every
# `<tag>.sql`, and the control-plane's assertRequiredMigrationIdentity requires the DB journal's
# hash set to equal that image-computed set. A Windows checkout / `git archive` with core.autocrlf
# would emit CRLF, drifting every hash away from the LF journal the DB was migrated with — which
# makes the CP try to re-apply already-applied migrations and FAIL BOOT on
# distributed_execution_migration_identity (observed on a manual staging deploy). Pin LF so the
# migration identity is byte-identical on every platform. The blobs are already LF, so this
# changes no committed bytes — it stops a Windows working tree / archive from diverging.
packages/db/src/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf
packages/db/src/migrations/meta/*.json text eol=lf
